NNAAMMEE
       restart_syscall  -  restart  a system call after interruption by a stop
       signal

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       lloonngg rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll((vvooiidd));;

       _N_o_t_e: There is no glibc wrapper for this system call; see NOTES.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll() system call is used  to  restart  certain  system
       calls  after  a  process that was stopped by a signal (e.g., SSIIGGSSTTOOPP or
       SSIIGGTTSSTTPP) is later resumed after receiving a SSIIGGCCOONNTT signal.  This  sys‐
       tem call is designed only for internal use by the kernel.

       rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll()  is used for restarting only those system calls that,
       when restarted,  should  adjust  their  time‐related  parameters—namely
       ppoollll(2)   (since   Linux   2.6.24),  nnaannoosslleeeepp(2)  (since  Linux  2.6),
       cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp(2) (since Linux 2.6), and ffuutteexx(2), when employed  with
       the  FFUUTTEEXX__WWAAIITT (since Linux 2.6.22) and FFUUTTEEXX__WWAAIITT__BBIITTSSEETT (since Linux
       2.6.31) operations.  rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll() restarts the interrupted  system
       call  with a time argument that is suitably adjusted to account for the
       time that has already elapsed (including the time where the process was
       stopped  by  a  signal).   Without  the  rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll()   mechanism,
       restarting  these  system  calls would not correctly deduct the already
       elapsed time when the process continued execution.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The return value of rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll() is the return value  of  whatever
       system call is being restarted.

EERRRROORRSS
       _e_r_r_n_o  is  set  as  per  the  errors  for whatever system call is being
       restarted by rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll().

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       Linux.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       Linux 2.6.

NNOOTTEESS
       There is no glibc wrapper for this system call, because it is  intended
       for use only by the kernel and should never be called by applications.

       The  kernel uses rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll() to ensure that when a system call is
       restarted after a process has been stopped by a signal and then resumed
       by SSIIGGCCOONNTT, then the time that the process spent in the  stopped  state
       is  counted against the timeout interval specified in the original sys‐
       tem call.  In the case of system calls that take a timeout argument and
       automatically restart after a stop signal plus SSIIGGCCOONNTT,  but  which  do
       not  have  the  rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll()  mechanism  built in, then, after the
       process resumes execution, the time that the process spent in the  stop
       state  is  _n_o_t  counted against the timeout value.  Notable examples of
       system calls that suffer this problem are ppppoollll(2), sseelleecctt(2), and ppssee‐‐
       lleecctt(2).

       From user space, the operation of rreessttaarrtt__ssyyssccaallll() is largely  invisi‐
       ble: to the process that made the system call that is restarted, it ap‐
       pears  as  though  that  system call executed and returned in the usual
       fashion.

